# Flight Booking Approval Workflow

This Livebook uses the complete `02_09` example. You will search for a flight,
select one offer, inspect the approval Directive, and submit one booking through
a local adapter.

The example has no network calls and needs no credentials. Run each cell in
order.

## Install Jido

Use the current Jido checkout and Kino for the Mermaid diagram.

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, path: Path.expand("../../..", __DIR__)},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
])
```

## Load The Example

The files stay beside the example. This cell loads the existing modules. The
notebook does not copy the Agent, Flow, Actions, Plugin, ports, or adapters.

```elixir
example_files = [
  "ports.ex",
  "support/adapters.ex",
  "booking_plugin.ex",
  "booking_actions.ex",
  "search_flow.ex",
  "approval_workflow.ex"
]

unless Code.ensure_loaded?(Jido.Examples.ApprovalWorkflow) do
  Enum.each(example_files, fn file ->
    Code.require_file(Path.join(__DIR__, file))
  end)
end

alias Jido.AgentServer, as: Server
alias Jido.Examples.ApprovalWorkflow, as: Example

alias Jido.Examples.ApprovalWorkflow.{
  FakeBookingAPI,
  FixtureSearch
}
```

## See The Commit Boundary

Each public command starts one Agent Turn. Search and selection commit state.
Approval commits `:submitting` state and a typed Directive. Plugin dispatch can
start only after that commit. The provider result returns as a Signal and starts
a later Turn.

```elixir
Kino.Mermaid.new("""
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor Developer
    participant Server as AgentServer
    participant Flow as SearchFlow
    participant Plugin as BookingPlugin
    participant API as BookingAPI

    Developer->>Server: search_flights(constraints, search context)
    Server->>Flow: validate, search, and prepare options
    Flow-->>Server: complete candidate state
    Server->>Server: commit awaiting_selection
    Server-->>Developer: searched Agent

    Developer->>Server: select_fare(option, revision, passenger)
    Server->>Server: commit awaiting_approval
    Server-->>Developer: selected Agent

    Developer->>Server: approve_booking(booking context)
    Server->>Server: commit submitting + SubmitBooking
    Server-->>Developer: submitting Agent
    Note over Server,Plugin: Directive dispatch starts after the state commit
    Server->>Plugin: dispatch SubmitBooking
    Plugin->>API: book(request, idempotency_key)
    API-->>Plugin: ok, booking_id
    Plugin-->>Server: booking_succeeded Signal
    Server->>Server: later Turn commits booked
""")
```

## Start The Local Runtime

The example keeps adapters in Turn context. It does not put the adapter PID in
Agent state, a Signal, or the Directive.

This setup cell is safe to run again. It stops the prior demo Agent before it
starts a new one. Livebook owns the local booking adapter process.

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()

agent_id = "approval-workflow-livebook"

case Jido.whereis_agent(agent_id) do
  nil -> :ok
  prior_server -> Jido.stop_agent(prior_server)
end

{:ok, booking_api} = Kino.start_child({FakeBookingAPI, []})
{:ok, server} = Jido.start_agent(Example, id: agent_id)

Server.snapshot(server)
```

The initial `state_version` is zero. The Agent starts with `:idle` booking
status.

## Search For Flights

`FixtureSearch` is a deterministic implementation of the search port. Replace
the fixture data to try other inputs. The Flow validates the request, calls the
port, filters the offers, sorts them, and returns the complete next Agent state.

```elixir
constraints = %{
  origin: "ORD",
  destination: "LAX",
  date: "2026-10-01",
  max_price: 400
}

offers = [
  %{
    id: "fare-2",
    origin: "ORD",
    destination: "LAX",
    date: "2026-10-01",
    price: 350,
    fare_revision: "r2"
  },
  %{
    id: "fare-1",
    origin: "ORD",
    destination: "LAX",
    date: "2026-10-01",
    price: 300,
    fare_revision: "r1"
  }
]

{:ok, searched} =
  Example.search_flights(server,
    input: %{constraints: constraints},
    context: %{search: {FixtureSearch, {:ok, offers}}}
  )

%{
  status: searched.state.booking_status,
  search_revision: searched.state.search_revision,
  option_ids: Enum.map(searched.state.offered_options, & &1.id),
  state_version: Server.snapshot(server).state_version
}
```

The expected status is `:awaiting_selection`. The lower-cost `fare-1` is first,
and the state version is one.

## Select One Current Offer

Selection includes the search revision. A stale client cannot select an offer
from an older search result.

```elixir
{:ok, selected} =
  Example.select_fare(
    server,
    "fare-1",
    searched.state.search_revision,
    "passenger-ref"
  )

%{
  status: selected.state.booking_status,
  approval: selected.state.approval,
  selected_offer: selected.state.selection.id,
  state_version: Server.snapshot(server).state_version
}
```

The expected status is `:awaiting_approval`. The state version is two.

## Preview Approval Without Dispatch

A direct command evaluates the same route against an Agent value. It returns a
candidate Agent and its Directives, but it does not commit live state or call the
provider. This is useful when you test the pure command boundary.

```elixir
booking_context = %{booking_adapter: {FakeBookingAPI, booking_api}}
{:ok, approval_signal} = Example.approve_booking_signal()

{:ok, candidate, [directive]} =
  Example.cmd(selected, approval_signal, context: booking_context)

%{
  live_status: Server.agent(server).state.booking_status,
  candidate_status: candidate.state.booking_status,
  directive_type: directive.__struct__,
  directive: Map.from_struct(directive),
  provider_calls: FakeBookingAPI.calls(booking_api)
}
```

The live status stays `:awaiting_approval`. The candidate status is
`:submitting`, and the provider call list stays empty. The Directive contains
portable request data and one stable idempotency key.

## Commit And Dispatch Approval

The live command commits the candidate. The Agent Server then dispatches the
Directive through `BookingPlugin`. The Plugin calls the booking port and casts a
correlated result Signal back to the Agent Server.

```elixir
{:ok, submitting} =
  Example.approve_booking(server, context: booking_context)

terminal_agent =
  Enum.reduce_while(1..100, nil, fn _attempt, _last_agent ->
    current_agent = Server.agent(server)

    if current_agent.state.booking_status in [:booked, :failed] do
      {:halt, current_agent}
    else
      Process.sleep(10)
      {:cont, current_agent}
    end
  end)

if terminal_agent.state.booking_status not in [:booked, :failed] do
  raise "booking did not reach a terminal state"
end

%{
  approval_returned: submitting.state.booking_status,
  terminal_status: terminal_agent.state.booking_status,
  booking_id: terminal_agent.state.booking_id,
  provider_calls: FakeBookingAPI.calls(booking_api),
  state_version: Server.snapshot(server).state_version
}
```

The approval call returns the committed `:submitting` Agent. The correlated
result Signal completes a later Turn and changes live status to `:booked`. The
state version is four: search, selection, approval, and provider result.

The bounded wait reads only the public Agent API. It is notebook coordination,
not part of the example Agent or Action code.

## Verify Duplicate Protection

The Agent accepts approval only from `:awaiting_approval`. A repeated approval
fails before it creates another provider request.

```elixir
{:error, duplicate_error} =
  Example.approve_booking(server, context: booking_context)

%{
  error: Jido.Flow.Error.to_map(duplicate_error),
  provider_call_count: length(FakeBookingAPI.calls(booking_api)),
  live_status: Server.agent(server).state.booking_status
}
```

The provider call count stays one, and live status stays `:booked`.

## Stop The Example

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(server)
:ok = Kino.terminate_child(booking_api)
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

This example does not provide durable Directive delivery, provider retry, or
recovery after process loss. Read the
[source README](README.md) and the
[behavior tests](../../../test/examples/02_workflow/02_09_approval_workflow/approval_workflow_test.exs)
for the failure and stale-result cases.
